---
import '@fontsource/della-respira';
import '@fontsource/josefin-sans/400.css';
import '@fontsource/josefin-sans/500.css';
import '../../styles/global.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="Elio’s Ocean Investigation — an illustrated Salon Format learning experience case study." />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href={`${base}brand/salon-format-mark.png`} />
    <title>Elio’s Ocean Investigation — Salon Format</title>
  </head>
  <body class="case-page elio-case">
    <header class="case-header">
      <a class="case-home" href="https://salonformat.com/" aria-label="Back to Salon Format home"><img src={`${base}brand/salon-format-mark.png`} alt="" /><span>Salon Format</span></a>
      <a href={`${base}#projects`}>All projects</a>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="case-hero">
        <div class="case-hero-copy">
          <p class="project-status">Interactive learning experience</p>
          <h1>Elio’s Ocean<br /><em>Investigation</em></h1>
          <p class="case-deck">A child’s drawing becomes a journey through ocean habitats, animal senses and food.</p>
          <div class="case-actions">
        <a class="case-button" href="https://ocean.salonformat.com/en/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the experience <span class="ui-arrow ui-arrow-external" aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="elio-case-art" aria-hidden="true">
          <img class="elio-ocean-backdrop" src={`${base}images/elio/ocean-collage.png`} alt="" />
          <span></span>
          <img src={`${base}images/elio/elios-wesen.png`} alt="" />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-intro">
        <p class="case-number">The starting point</p>
        <h2>Could this creature really live in the ocean?</h2>
        <p>Elio was four when he drew an imaginary creature and decided it lived in the ocean. The experience takes that idea seriously and turns it into a question learners can investigate.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="case-process elio-process">
        <div class="process-heading">
          <p class="case-number">Experience flow</p>
          <h2>Explore.<br />Investigate.<br />Create.</h2>
        </div>
        <ol class="journey-list">
          <li><span>Enter the ocean</span><p>Move through an illustrated underwater environment and notice what changes with depth.</p></li>
          <li><span>Meet the question</span><p>Use Elio’s creature as the starting point for a real investigation.</p></li>
          <li><span>Look for clues</span><p>Consider habitat, senses and food to decide what an ocean animal needs.</p></li>
          <li><span>Test the creature</span><p>Connect each observation back to the original drawing.</p></li>
          <li><span>Draw your own</span><p>Create a new creature and give it features that suit its ocean home.</p></li>
          <li><span>Run an ocean check</span><p>Review the design through the same questions used during the journey.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="case-image-section elio-image-section">
        <figure class="historical-figure">
          <img src={`${base}images/elio/twilight-world.png`} alt="Illustrated twilight ocean habitat from Elio’s Ocean Investigation" />
          <figcaption>One of the illustrated ocean environments explored during the investigation.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="case-image-copy">
          <p class="case-number">The learning environment</p>
          <h2>Science begins with curiosity.</h2>
          <p>The visual world makes the ocean feel worth exploring, while the questions give learners a clear way to examine what they see.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="decision-section elio-decisions">
        <p class="case-number">Design choices</p>
        <div class="decision-grid">
          <article><h3>A real drawing</h3><p>Elio’s original creature remains at the centre, so the learning begins with a child’s own idea.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Questions before answers</h3><p>The experience invites learners to notice, compare and decide before explaining the science.</p></article>
          <article><h3>An illustrated journey</h3><p>Layered ocean scenes turn movement through depth into part of the learning experience.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Room to participate</h3><p>The final drawing and ocean check let learners apply the investigation to a creature of their own.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-role elio-role">
        <p class="case-number">Role and contribution</p>
        <h2>Learning Experience Design · Concept · Writing · Visual Direction · Interaction Design · Prototyping</h2>
        <p>I developed the learning journey, narrative, visual direction and interactions around Elio’s original drawing. The public version is available in German, English and French, with controls for sound and motion.</p>
      </section>

      <nav class="case-next" aria-label="Project navigation">
        <p>Next case study</p>
        <a href={`${base}projects/infinite`}>Into the Infinite <span class="ui-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
      </nav>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
